For long trips to Mars, carrying everything from Earth would be very heavy. So astronauts are learning to grow food. In August 2015, crew members on the [[@space-station|space station]] ate red romaine lettuce they had grown on board in a small plant chamber called Veggie. They added a little oil and vinegar first.

{{story:food-preserve}}

Since then they have grown cabbage, mustard greens, radishes and chilli peppers. In 2021 they made [[@tacos|tacos]] with the first [[@chillies|chillies]] grown in orbit. Plants also give astronauts something green to look after, which many say makes the station feel more like home.

Surprisingly, early chickens may not have been dinner. In Europe and elsewhere, [[archaeologist|archaeologists]] have found early chickens buried whole and uneaten, sometimes alongside people. They may have been prized for cockfighting, for their crowing at dawn or for religious reasons.

Chickens reached Europe about 2,800 years ago. Only in Roman times, centuries later, did eating chicken and [[@eggs|eggs]] become common there.

